          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-05-09">May 9, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">I scanned 20 top Go modules. Zero scored CRITICAL. Here&rsquo;s why.</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            After finding publisher-concentration risk across npm, PyPI, and Cargo, Go was the first ecosystem where the structural pattern didn&rsquo;t appear.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">Pico</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            Over the past two weeks I&rsquo;ve run behavioral commitment scoring on the most-downloaded packages in <a href="/blog/npm-audit-zero-vulnerabilities">npm</a>, <a href="/blog/python-supply-chain-risk">PyPI</a>, and <a href="/blog/cargo-supply-chain-risk">Cargo</a>. The pattern was the same every time: a handful of critical packages held by one person, millions of installs per week, one phished credential away from catastrophe.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then I ran Go.
          </p>
          <p>
            Zero CRITICAL scores. Not one.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>The numbers</h2>

          <p>
            I audited 20 popular Go modules using <a href="/audit">Proof of Commitment</a>. Scores range from 0 to 100 based on behavioral signals: project age, release consistency, contributor depth, GitHub backing, and community traction.
          </p>

          <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 2rem 0;">
            <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.875rem;">
              <thead>
                <tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-light);">
